sri lanka's interim government has  reimposed a curfew a day after angry  crowds stormed government buildings the  prime minister who is now acting  president has ordered security forces to  restore calm  protesters have agreed to leave  government buildings as people still  wait for the official resignation of  president gotabaya rajapaksa he fled the  country while it is going through a  devastating economic crisis  and we are now joined by oliver meyer  who is the south asia correspondent for  german public broadcaster aid he is in  colomba colombo the sri lankan capital  oliver how's the situation in the  capital right now it looks quite  peaceful  yeah well it's certainly way more calm  today um before there was a hectic day  yesterday uh people were protesting in  front of the office of the prime  minister  the police used tear gas yesterday  evening we were at the parliament also  lots of tear gas coming from the police  lots of wounded people and today i just  talked to protesters they said it's uh  like the quiet before the storm i mean  the situation is still that the  president hasn't really resigned the  prime minister has taken over as an  acting president and people are still  very very mad and they demand a  resignation right away that's what they  want and they say they have to gather  their strength now for the next upcoming  demonstration if the president and the  prime minister don't resign in the  upcoming days  well the prime minister said that he  will resign so what's the holdup  where is the president now and when can  we expect the formal resignation  [Music]  yeah lots of questions where i don't  have definite answers to the fact is  that president gotta gotta buy a richard  paxa flew to the maldives yesterday  which is not his final destination as  sources say his final destinations may  either be singapore or saudi arabia  that's probably where he's heading to  and he said before he hasn't reached his  final destination he will not resign and  that's what makes protesters so angry  and  pretty much the same as for prime  minister rani vikram singh  he said he's going to resign when a new  government is being built and that's  probably going to be in next wednesday  but  it's also a thing that people are not  very sure about here and he also might  be running as a president as well which  people do not want at all  the people want to see both gotabaya  rajapaksa  right away actually  now there are shortages of fuel food and  medicine among other necessities how are  ordinary sri lankans coping  very very tough for all of these people  we talked to many of these people for  example just a few days ago we talked to  a guy who has been standing with us tuk  tuk in front of a line of a fuel station  for seven days and seven nights he  hasn't really left the queue slept in  his tuk tuk and it's really rough for  him and his family because his family is  home his wife is sick he can hardly  provide any medicine he has no fuel to  drive her to the hospital and everyone  is just pretty much struggling to  survive they cannot buy food because  food has gotten really expensive  medicine is not their fuel as i already  mentioned is not there so pretty much  everyone especially the poorest are  really struggling right now in this  country  oliver meyer there reporting from  colombo thank you very much oliver  now sri lanka is descending into chaos  and parliament has been unable to enact  reforms i'm now joined by harini amara  surya a parliamentarian for the national  people's power party can i ask you as an  opposition politician are you powerless  to change this situation at all  uh no we hope not tomorrow parliament is  convened and when parliament is convened  we hope to  move ahead with the appointment of uh  a president  as as agreed at the party leaders  meeting that was convened by the speaker  of the parliament uh several days ago  that on the 20th of this month a  parliament will  elect a new president  currently there is some uh confusion  as to the status of the acting president  because whether how legal that  appointment has been because there is no  official communication from gojabe  rajapaksa who as you know uh left the  country hurriedly and whose whereabouts  ever since uh have been uh unclear  do you think that you as as opposition  have the support of the people on the  street  i think as the opposition the people of  on the street expect us to do a  particular task and that is to ensure  that  uh an interim government is appointed  and that parliament is dissolved as soon  as possible so that fresh elections can  be held  we have been arguing and protesters  outside have also been saying this that  parliament has also lost its legitimacy  now  and that uh what do you say to them  are the other protesters right there has  parliament lost its legitimacy  yes one of the demands of the part of  the protest has been for uh against the  parliament as well because the  parliament as it's currently constituted  uh has a two-thirds majority from the  president's party which has made it  really difficult uh to push through any  kinds of reforms that people have been  demanding and as a result uh there's a  huge sort of trust deficit between uh  the parliament and and uh  the people outside especially the  protesters  uh and and we've been arguing for a  while that this whole crisis  uh the economic crisis has been  precipitated by a deep political crisis  which also needs to be addressed or  which needs to be addressed in in order  to ensure some kind of stability  and for that we are we are asking for a  for a fresh election  okay but are you sure you mentioned a  trust deficit uh between the public uh  and parliament but can you promise any  real change for the country if the  opposition comes to power  well  the the question is that if if there is  an election the people will then select  a party and a set of representatives of  their choice and there will be a period  that will be given to that new  government to that new administration to  to  to make the kinds of interventions or to  bring about the necessary reforms  uh  so that that building of confidence and  that and that um trust  that that will come with a new  government we think is essential we are  not we are not predicting who that new  government is will be but we think that  of election will give a fresh start  uh for a government and a and a new  beginning for for for the people also to  be able to move forward with some kind  of reforms  harini amara surya there parliamentarian  from sri lanka thank you very much for  your time
 

